Lung Cancer (NSCLC/SCLC)
Haematology and Oncology
Persistent chronic cough/haemoptysis, Unintentional weight loss, Hoarseness/superior vena cava syndrome, Solid lung mass on CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-029
Reason for Visit: Rapid development of profound facial swelling, severe dyspnoea, and haemoptysis requiring urgent assessment for airway compromise and malignant compression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an older patient with a chronic cough who rapidly developed a swollen, purple face and severe shortness of breath over the past 12 hours. Urgent in-person evaluation was mandated to diagnose Superior Vena Cava (SVC) syndrome and assess for impending airway collapse, as delaying intervention until normal clinic hours posed an immediate, life-threatening risk of suffocation and cerebral oedema.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: They state they cannot breathe, their face feels incredibly tight and swollen, and they are coughing up streaks of blood.
History of Present Illness: The 65-year-old patient, a heavy smoker, reports a 4-month history of a persistent chronic cough and an unexplained 8 kg unintentional weight loss. Over the past 48 hours, they noted progressive exertional dyspnoea and a hoarse, raspy voice. Over the last 12 hours, this escalated to severe dyspnoea at rest, profound facial swelling, and a sensation of intense pressure in the head. They have also produced recurrent blood-streaked sputum tonight. They complain of deep, dull, localized chest pain unassociated with movement.
Functional Status: Sitting rigidly upright in a chair; unable to lie flat or speak in full sentences due to dyspnoea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending airway obstruction and extreme patient distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 26 | SpO2: 91 percent on room air | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Acutely distressed, tachypnoeic adult. Pronounced plethora and severe oedema of the face and neck.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular/Neck: Markedly distended, non-pulsatile jugular veins are visible bilaterally, failing to collapse on inspiration. Pronounced superficial collateral veins are visible across the anterior chest wall.
Respiratory: Tachypnoeic with increased work of breathing. Auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds and stony dullness to percussion at the right lung base, indicating a pleural effusion.
HEENT: Noticeable hoarseness of the voice due to presumed recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement. Periorbital oedema is prominent.
Extremities: Upper extremity oedema is present bilaterally; lower extremities are entirely normal.
Environmental Observations: Tissues stained with fresh red blood.
Medication Reconciliation: Using a salbutamol inhaler repeatedly with absolute zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lung Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Superior Vena Cava Syndrome)
Differentials:

Severe Allergic Reaction (Anaphylaxis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the insidious progressive timeline, the presence of localized stony dullness (effusion), weight loss, and haemoptysis, rather than acute urticaria and wheeze.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the oedema being strictly isolated to the face, neck, and upper limbs (classic SVC obstruction) with completely normal lower extremities.

Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the rapid onset of mechanical venous obstruction (SVC syndrome) and the specific hoarseness (nerve palsy), which strongly point to an expanding neoplastic mass.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The rapid onset of facial plethora, upper extremity oedema, and distended neck veins in a patient with chronic cough, weight loss, and haemoptysis is pathognomonic for Superior Vena Cava (SVC) syndrome, typically caused by a rapidly expanding central lung mass (like SCLC). The concurrent hoarseness indicates direct mediastinal invasion. The patient's resting hypoxia (SpO2 91 percent) and tachypnoea represent critical respiratory compromise. Delaying this assessment would expose the patient to fatal airway obstruction, cerebral oedema, and asphyxiation overnight. The urgent home visit was essential to recognize the malignant mechanical obstruction and initiate immediate emergency transport for life-saving radiotherapy or stenting.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate dispatch of a priority ambulance for urgent Emergency Department transfer. Administered high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask to maintain SpO2 above 94 percent. Handover provided for urgent contrast-enhanced chest CT (anticipating a distinct solid lung mass/hilar adenopathy compressing the SVC) and urgent oncology/radiation review.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain seated strictly upright and do not attempt to lie back under any circumstances. By keeping the torso vertical, gravity prevents further blood from pooling in the head and neck, slightly relieving the intense pressure on the airway and brain.

Focus strictly on slow, controlled exhales rather than gasping in. By controlling the breath rate, you prevent dynamic airway collapse and improve the efficiency of oxygen exchange in the remaining open lung tissue.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer is required. If the patient loses consciousness or if breathing completely stops prior to paramedic arrival, the spouse must initiate basic life support positioning and update the emergency dispatcher.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe facial swelling and breathing difficulty strongly suggest a large mass pressing on the major blood vessels in the chest; immediate hospital scans are required to confirm this.
Clinical Course: The blockage is preventing blood from draining out of the head and arms, causing the swelling and shortness of breath. The hospital will use targeted treatments to shrink the mass and relieve the pressure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Wait for the paramedics. Do not try to stand or walk. Keep sitting straight up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A growth in the lung is pressing on the main vein that returns blood from the head to the heart. This acts like a kink in a hose, causing fluid to back up into the face and airway. The ambulance will provide oxygen, and the hospital will perform urgent scans to plan emergency treatment to unblock the vessel.
Actionable Steps: Stay completely upright. Breathe slowly. Let the paramedics handle all physical movement.

